From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Satya Benson <satya@benson.earth>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:48:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 42/93] soc: apple: mcc: let the T6050 system level cache
 allocate

The T6050 memory cache controller has up to 64 planes: eight AMCCs
of four planes per die, on up to two dies. The binding already
describes this layout, but apple-mcc has no match for the controller.
The boot loader leaves the ways enabled and powered with allocation
disabled, so a miss in an agent's own caches goes to DRAM.

T6050 uses the T8140 sequence: check each plane's ways and flush
engine, clear the allocation-disable word and read it back. On
shutdown, removal or deep sleep, disable allocation and flush. Add
the match and raise MCC_MAX_PLANES to the binding's limit of 64.

Wait for a driver to take over every client the DT lists in
apple,boot-dma-devices. A disabled display node can still be scanning
out the boot framebuffer, so its status cannot establish that it is
safe to enable allocation. The loader must drop a client from the
list only once it knows that client's boot DMA is quiescent.

Based on #896 by Jeff Queisser, which measured the effect on J775D.

Signed-off-by: Satya Benson <satya@benson.earth>
---
 drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig | 12 ++++++------
 drivers/soc/apple/mcc.c   |  5 ++++-
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
index 8dc9384bdc5..9e5bcc23ef4 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/Kconfig
@@ -47,17 +47,17 @@ config APPLE_MAILBOX
 	  Say Y here if you have an Apple SoC.
 
 config APPLE_MCC
-	tristate "Apple T8140 and T6040 system level cache"
+	tristate "Apple T8140, T6040 and T6050 system level cache"
 	depends on ARCH_APPLE || COMPILE_TEST
 	depends on OF
 	default ARCH_APPLE
 	help
-	  The boot firmware of the Apple T8140 and T6040 SoCs enables the
-	  system level cache without letting it allocate. This driver allows
-	  allocation once the devices that took over DMA from the bootloader
-	  are bound.
+	  The boot firmware of the Apple T8140, T6040 and T6050 SoCs enables
+	  the system level cache without letting it allocate. This driver
+	  allows allocation once the devices that took over DMA from the
+	  bootloader are bound.
 
-	  Say 'y' or 'm' here if you have an Apple T8140 or T6040 SoC.
+	  Say 'y' or 'm' here if you have an Apple T8140, T6040 or T6050 SoC.
 
 config APPLE_MCC_KUNIT_TEST
 	bool "KUnit tests for Apple MCC allocation and unwind" if !KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
diff --git a/drivers/soc/apple/mcc.c b/drivers/soc/apple/mcc.c
index ae15c4ea54e..dcc36d303f6 100644
--- a/drivers/soc/apple/mcc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/apple/mcc.c
@@ -33,7 +33,8 @@
 #define MCC_CACHE_FLUSH		0x2844
 #define MCC_CACHE_FLUSH_BUSY	BIT(0)
 
-#define MCC_MAX_PLANES		32
+/* T6050: eight AMCCs of four planes on each die, 64 planes on two dies. */
+#define MCC_MAX_PLANES		64
 #define MCC_FLUSH_TIMEOUT_US	100000
 
 struct apple_mcc {
@@ -146,6 +147,7 @@ static int apple_mcc_wait_boot_dma(struct device *dev)
 
 		if (pdev)
 			put_device(&pdev->dev);
+		/* A disabled DT node can still have DMA left running. */
 		if (!bound) {
 			ret = dev_err_probe(dev, -EPROBE_DEFER, "waiting for %pOF to bind\n",
 					    it.node);
@@ -387,6 +389,7 @@ static const struct of_device_id apple_mcc_of_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "apple,t8132-mcc" },
 	{ .compatible = "apple,t8140-mcc" },
 	{ .compatible = "apple,t8142-mcc" },
+	{ .compatible = "apple,t6050-mcc" },
 	{}
 };
 MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, apple_mcc_of_match);
